[{:db/id "q-340056/field-1/dfd546ecda603720133713f12f83959e6a3796b7861a74bd0e4fe9f71ed28784" :answer/id #uuid "cfc49b76-77a7-546d-a59c-0e938e14c9d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6e^x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340056/field-1/f171621256a3f9c66cb2aec5c21580416469af0dd617042778d3f8e27012772a" :answer/id #uuid "43073e84-5690-55fc-ac6a-fa934c6e7fba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6e^x+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340056/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "785fb0a2-54cf-5451-8622-411e9884a29a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-340056/field-1/dfd546ecda603720133713f12f83959e6a3796b7861a74bd0e4fe9f71ed28784" :answer-field/choices ["q-340056/field-1/dfd546ecda603720133713f12f83959e6a3796b7861a74bd0e4fe9f71ed28784" "q-340056/field-1/f171621256a3f9c66cb2aec5c21580416469af0dd617042778d3f8e27012772a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340056" :question/id #uuid "5a13ddf1-d3b9-5682-9ec2-7dea245fa36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340056" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡8) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =6{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340056/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340056"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340067/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "47a48e23-9c46-506c-8e92-391f4fe24039" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340067/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "304b2bbd-9e43-58d4-8a50-05bf8bb76d8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-340067/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer-field/choices ["q-340067/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340067" :question/id #uuid "020a6823-fb1e-518f-92c1-a5ae8eb08fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340067" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡4$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡4) \\\\  & =9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =9{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡2} & ={(9{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9{e}^{ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340067/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340067"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101587/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "2c8e8f7d-6782-50d3-aa4c-e69b9df8ef08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :d